    "topic": "Addition",
    "subtopic": "Mental addition facts",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Addition</p><h2>Mental addition facts</h2><p>Mental addition facts explains a specific idea inside Addition.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Mental addition facts</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Mental addition facts needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Mental addition facts</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Mental addition facts, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Mental addition facts with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Mental addition facts becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Practice small sums daily.</li><li>Start with doubles like 4 + 4.</li><li>Then learn make-ten facts like 6 + 4.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Mental addition facts connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write all pairs that make 10: 0 + 10, 1 + 9, and so on.</li><li>Practice saying them aloud.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555363
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 30,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__addition__addition-word-problems",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Addition",
    "subtopic": "Addition word problems",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Addition</p><h2>Addition word problems</h2><p>Addition word problems explains a specific idea inside Addition.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Addition word problems</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Addition word problems needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Addition word problems</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Addition word problems, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Addition word problems with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Addition word problems becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Ask: Are things coming together?</li><li>If yes, add.</li><li>Write the number sentence, like 3 + 2 = 5.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Addition word problems connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Make a story for 7 + 5.</li><li>Then solve this: A box has 9 red balls and 4 blue balls.</li><li>How many balls are there?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555363
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 31,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-as-taking-away",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction as taking away",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction as taking away</h2><p>Subtraction as taking away explains a specific idea inside Subtraction.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction as taking away</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction removes a part from a whole.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start with the total.</p>\n<p>Take away the given number.</p>\n<p>Count what remains.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The remaining objects are the total minus the removed objects.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it when spending money, eating biscuits, losing points, or giving away pencils.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may subtract from the smaller number.</p>\n<p>Always start with the whole.</p></section><section class=\"formula-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon calculator\"></span><h3>Formula</h3></div><p>a - b = left.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction tells how many remain after taking away.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Subtraction as taking away, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Subtraction as taking away but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction as taking away becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction as taking away</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Subtraction as taking away, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Subtraction as taking away with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction as taking away becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Take a group of objects.</li><li>Remove some.</li><li>Count the objects left.</li><li>For 7 - 2, remove 2 from 7 and count 5.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction as taking away connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use 12 buttons.</li><li>Take away 5.</li><li>Count how many are left.</li><li>Write the number sentence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555364
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 32,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-as-difference",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction as difference",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction as difference</h2><p>Subtraction as difference explains a specific idea inside Subtraction.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction as difference</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Difference tells the gap between two numbers.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Match objects from two groups.</p>\n<p>Count the extra objects in the bigger group.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Matched objects cancel each other, and the leftover part shows the difference.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We compare marks, heights, scores, money, and ages.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may take away without comparing.</p>\n<p>For difference, think about the gap.</p></section><section class=\"formula-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon calculator\"></span><h3>Formula</h3></div><p>bigger number - smaller number = difference.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Difference means the gap between two amounts.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Subtraction as difference, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Subtraction as difference but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction as difference becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction as difference</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Subtraction as difference, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Subtraction as difference with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction as difference becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Put two groups side by side.</li><li>Match one object from each group.</li><li>Count the extra objects left in the bigger group.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction as difference connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Compare 11 stars and 7 stars.</li><li>Draw matching pairs.</li><li>How many extra stars are there?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555364
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 33,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-within-5",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction within 5",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction within 5</h2><p>Subtraction within 5 explains a specific idea inside Subtraction.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction within 5</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Small subtraction finds what is left after taking away.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start with up to 5 objects.</p>\n<p>Remove some objects.</p>\n<p>Count the remaining objects.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Removing objects reduces the total.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it with fingers, toys, coins, biscuits, and small scores.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may count the removed objects as the answer.</p>\n<p>The answer is what remains.</p></section><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Example</h3></div><p>5 - 2 = 3.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction within 5 shows how small numbers become less.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Subtraction within 5, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Subtraction within 5 but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction within 5 becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction within 5</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Subtraction within 5, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Subtraction within 5 with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 5 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Use one hand.</li><li>Show 5 fingers.</li><li>Fold 2 fingers.</li><li>Count the fingers still up.</li><li>The answer is 3.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 5 connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Solve with fingers: 5 - 1, 4 - 2, 3 - 3.</li><li>Write the answers.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555365
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 34,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-within-10",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction within 10",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction within 10</h2><p>Subtraction within 10 means subtracting from numbers up to 10.</p>\n<p>It helps children use fingers, ten-frames, and number lines.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction within 10</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 10 finds the remaining amount from small numbers.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start with the bigger number.</p>\n<p>Take away the smaller number.</p>\n<p>Count what is left.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Taking away reduces the total by the number removed.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it in money, snacks, game points, and classroom objects.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may reverse the order, like doing 3 - 8.</p>\n<p>In Class 1, start with the larger total.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Start with the whole.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction within 10 finds what is left from numbers up to 10.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction within 10</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>10 chocolates, eat 4, left 6.</li><li>₹9, spend ₹5, left ₹4.</li><li>8 goals, remove 2, left 6.</li><li>Imagine a ten-frame with some boxes crossed out.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 10 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Show the first number with fingers or dots.</li><li>Cross out the number taken away.</li><li>Count the remaining dots.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 10 supports fact families and mental subtraction.</p>\n<p>It builds confidence before subtraction within 20.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use a ten-frame to solve 10 - 3, 9 - 4, and 7 - 5.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555365
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 35,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-within-20",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction within 20",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction within 20</h2><p>Subtraction within 20 means subtracting from numbers up to 20.</p>\n<p>It helps children handle teen numbers and simple real-life problems.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction within 20</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 20 finds how many remain or the difference.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Use objects, a number line, or break the number into tens and ones.</p>\n<p>Example: 13 - 5 can be 13 - 3 - 2 = 8.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Breaking numbers makes subtraction easier and keeps the value the same.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it for marks, money, pages, and game scores.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may forget to subtract all parts.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Take away to ten, then take the rest.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction within 20 becomes easier when you break numbers.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction within 20</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>15 sweets, eat 6, left 9.</li><li>₹18, spend ₹7, left ₹11.</li><li>14 runs needed, score 5, need 9 more.</li><li>It is like opening a ten-box and loose ones.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction within 20 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>For 16 - 7, take away 6 to reach 10.</li><li>Then take away 1 more.</li><li>The answer is 9.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This prepares for regrouping.</p>\n<p>Children learn that teen numbers are one ten and some ones.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Solve by making ten: 14 - 6, 17 - 8, 12 - 5.</li><li>Show each step.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555365
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 36,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-on-number-line",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction on number line",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction on number line</h2><p>Subtraction on number line explains a specific idea inside Subtraction.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction on number line</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A number line shows subtraction visually.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start at the first number.</p>\n<p>Jump backward the number being subtracted.</p>\n<p>The landing number is the answer.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Each jump backward reduces the number by one or more.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it for countdowns, floors, scores, and remaining distance.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may count the starting point as a jump.</p>\n<p>Start counting after moving.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Subtraction means jump left.</p>\n<p>Key Point: On a number line, subtraction moves backward.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Subtraction on number line, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Subtraction on number line but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtraction on number line becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction on number line</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Subtraction on number line, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Subtraction on number line with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction on number line becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Draw numbers in order.</li><li>Put your finger on 12.</li><li>For 12 - 5, jump five spaces left.</li><li>You land on 7.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction on number line connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw a number line from 0 to 20.</li><li>Show 15 - 6 using backward jumps.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555366
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 37,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__zero-in-subtraction",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Zero in subtraction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Zero in subtraction</h2><p>Zero in subtraction has two simple meanings.</p>\n<p>Subtracting zero changes nothing.</p>\n<p>Subtracting a number from itself gives zero.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Zero in subtraction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Zero can mean nothing is taken away or nothing is left.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>If you have 8 pencils and take away 0, you still have 8.</p>\n<p>If you have 8 and take away 8, none are left.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Removing nothing keeps the total.</p>\n<p>Removing all objects leaves no objects.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it in money, marks, scores, and object counting.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may think every subtraction with zero gives zero.</p></section><section class=\"formula-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon calculator\"></span><h3>Formula</h3></div><p>a - 0 = a, and a - a = 0.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Subtracting zero keeps the number, but subtracting all gives zero.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Zero in subtraction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>₹10 - ₹0 = ₹10.</li><li>6 biscuits - 6 biscuits = 0 biscuits.</li><li>9 runs needed, 0 scored, still 9 needed.</li><li>Zero is like taking nothing from a box.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Zero in subtraction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>If you take away nothing, the amount stays.</li><li>If you take away everything, zero remains.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>These zero facts help children understand identity and inverse ideas.</p>\n<p>They also prevent common calculation mistakes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Solve: 7 - 0, 12 - 0, 5 - 5, 9 - 9.</li><li>Explain both zero rules.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555366
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 38,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__fact-families",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Fact families",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Fact families</h2><p>Fact families are related addition and subtraction facts using the same numbers.</p>\n<p>They help children see how addition and subtraction are connected.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fact families</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Three numbers can make related facts.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>If 4 + 3 = 7, then 3 + 4 = 7, 7 - 4 = 3, and 7 - 3 = 4.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Addition joins parts to make a whole.</p>\n<p>Subtraction starts with the whole and finds a part.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Fact families help check sums, solve missing numbers, and do mental math.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may use a number outside the family.</p>\n<p>Use only the same three numbers.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Two parts and one whole make a family.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Fact families connect addition and subtraction.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fact families</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>5, 2, and 7 make four facts.</li><li>6 apples and 4 apples make 10 apples.</li><li>₹8 and ₹2 make ₹10, so ₹10 - ₹8 = ₹2.</li><li>It is like the same family standing in different orders.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fact families becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Find the two parts and the whole.</li><li>Write two addition facts and two subtraction facts using them.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Fact families prepare children for inverse operations.</p>\n<p>An inverse operation undoes another operation.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Make a fact family using 3, 5, and 8.</li><li>Write all four number sentences.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555366
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 39,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__subtraction__subtraction-word-problems",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Subtraction",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction word problems",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Subtraction</p><h2>Subtraction word problems</h2><p>Subtraction word problems explains a specific idea inside Subtraction.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Subtraction word problems</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Subtraction word problems needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Subtraction word problems</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Subtraction word problems, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Subtraction word problems with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Subtraction word problems becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Ask: Is something removed or compared?</li><li>If yes, subtract.</li><li>Write the number sentence and answer with a word.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Subtraction word problems connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Solve: Ria has 14 stickers.</li><li>She gives 5 to a friend.</li><li>How many stickers are left?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555367
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 40,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__circle",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Circle",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>A circle</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>Every point on its edge is equally far from the middle. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It is useful when looking at wheels, coins, clocks, plates, and buttons.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>A <b>circle</b> is a round flat shape. It has no corners and no straight sides.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to recognise it</font></h3><ol><li>Look for a smooth round edge.</li><li>Check that there are <b>no corners</b>.</li><li>Find the middle point in your mind.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>A circle stays round because all points on the edge are the same distance from the centre.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Wheels on a bicycle</li><li>Coins and buttons</li><li>Clock faces</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Calling an oval a circle.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> A circle is equally wide in every direction. An oval is longer one way.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> A circle is a round 2D shape with no corners.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> A coin looks like a circle.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> An egg shape is not a circle because it is longer on one side.</li><li><b>Use:</b> A wheel is circular so it rolls smoothly.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>Think of a circle like a <b>coin</b>. It is round all around. Your finger can move along its edge without stopping at any corner.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>In geometry, a circle is the set of all points at a fixed distance from one point called the <b>centre</b>. That fixed distance is called the <b>radius</b>.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Find three circle-shaped objects at home. Draw one. Then explain why it is a circle and not an oval.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 41,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__triangle",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Triangle",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>A triangle</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It has exactly three straight sides and three corners. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It is useful when seeing roof shapes, flags, slices of pizza, and warning signs.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>A <b>triangle</b> is a flat shape made from <b>3 straight sides</b>.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to recognise it</font></h3><ol><li>Count the sides.</li><li>Check that all sides are straight.</li><li>Count the corners. A triangle has <b>3 corners</b>.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>Three straight sides can close to make one simple flat shape. That closed shape is a triangle.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Road warning signs</li><li>Pizza slices</li><li>Strong bridge supports</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Calling any pointed shape a triangle.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> A triangle must have exactly three straight sides.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> A triangle is a closed 2D shape with 3 straight sides.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> A pizza slice can look triangular.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> A shape with 4 sides is not a triangle.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Triangles make frames strong because the sides support each other.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>A triangle is like a <b>three-stick frame</b>. If the three sticks join end to end and close, you see a triangle.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>Every triangle is a polygon. A <b>polygon</b> is a closed flat shape made only from straight line segments.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Draw three shapes. One should be a triangle. One should have four sides. One should be open. Circle only the triangle and explain your choice.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 42,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__square",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Square",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>A square</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It has four equal sides and four square corners. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It helps children recognise tiles, windows, game boards, and boxes drawn from the front.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>A <b>square</b> is a flat shape with <b>4 equal sides</b> and <b>4 right corners</b>.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to recognise it</font></h3><ol><li>Count 4 sides.</li><li>Check all sides look equal.</li><li>Check each corner looks like the corner of a book.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>Equal sides make the shape balanced. Right corners make opposite sides line up neatly.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Floor tiles</li><li>Chessboard boxes</li><li>Sticky notes</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Thinking a tilted square is not a square.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> Turning a square does not change its side lengths or corners.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> A square has 4 equal sides and 4 right corners.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> One chessboard box is a square.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> A rectangle may have unequal long and short sides.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Square tiles fit neatly because their sides match.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>A square is like a <b>perfect tile</b>. All four sides are the same size, and all four corners stand straight.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>A square is both a <b>rectangle</b> and a <b>rhombus</b>. It has right angles like a rectangle and equal sides like a rhombus.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Draw a square. Turn your paper sideways. Is it still a square? Explain using sides and corners.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 43,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__rectangle",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Rectangle",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>A rectangle</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It has four straight sides and four square corners. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It helps children recognise books, doors, mobile screens, boards, and tables.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>A <b>rectangle</b> is a flat shape with <b>4 sides</b> and <b>4 right corners</b>.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to recognise it</font></h3><ol><li>Count 4 sides.</li><li>Look for two long sides and two short sides.</li><li>Check that every corner is square.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>Opposite sides of a rectangle match. Right corners keep the shape straight and neat.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Mobile phone screen</li><li>Classroom board</li><li>Book cover</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Thinking a square is never a rectangle.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> A square is a special rectangle because it also has 4 right corners.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> A rectangle has 4 sides and 4 right corners.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> A book cover is usually a rectangle.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> A circle has no straight sides, so it is not a rectangle.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Doors are rectangular so they fit straight frames.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>A rectangle is like a <b>door shape</b>. It has two matching long sides, two matching short sides, and four neat corners.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>A rectangle is a quadrilateral. A <b>quadrilateral</b> is any closed flat shape with four sides.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Find a rectangle in your room. Count its sides and corners. Then tell whether it is also a square.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 44,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__sorting-2d-shapes",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Sorting 2D shapes",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>Sorting 2D shapes</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It means grouping flat shapes by properties such as sides, corners, or curved edges. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It helps children compare shapes instead of only memorising names.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p><b>Sorting</b> means putting similar shapes together.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to sort</font></h3><ol><li>Choose one rule, such as number of sides.</li><li>Look at each shape carefully.</li><li>Put shapes with the same property in one group.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>Shapes can be compared using clear properties. A property is something we can check, like <b>3 sides</b> or <b>no corners</b>.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Sorting blocks in class</li><li>Choosing tiles</li><li>Finding matching game pieces</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Sorting only by colour.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> In geometry, sort by shape properties first.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> Sort 2D shapes by clear properties like sides and corners.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> Put circles in one group and triangles in another.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> A red circle and blue circle still belong together by shape.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Builders sort tiles by shape before placing them.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>Sorting shapes is like sorting toys. Put the ones with the same shape-rule together, even if their colours are different.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>Sorting introduces <b>classification</b>. Classification means grouping objects using shared properties. This prepares students for sets and geometry definitions.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Draw a circle, square, rectangle, and triangle. Sort them into two groups: shapes with corners and shapes without corners.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 45,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__shape-patterns",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Shape patterns",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>A shape pattern</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It is a repeated order of shapes. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It helps children predict what comes next in designs, tiles, games, and decorations.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>A <b>pattern</b> repeats in a planned way.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How to continue it</font></h3><ol><li>Look for the smallest repeating part.</li><li>Name the order of shapes.</li><li>Repeat the same order to find the next shape.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>The next shape follows the same rule because the pattern repeats. If the rule changes, it is not the same pattern.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Floor tiles</li><li>Gift wrapping</li><li>Classroom borders</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Looking only at the last shape.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> Find the repeating group first, then continue it.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> A shape pattern repeats the same shape order.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> Circle, square, circle, square comes next with circle.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> Circle, square, triangle, circle does not follow the same two-shape rule.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Tile designs often repeat shapes.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>A shape pattern is like a song beat. Once you hear the repeated beat, you can tell what comes next.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>A repeating pattern has a <b>unit of repeat</b>. For circle, square, circle, square, the unit is circle-square.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Continue this pattern: circle, triangle, square, circle, triangle, square, __. Explain the repeating unit.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 46,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__2d-shapes__combining-shapes",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "2D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Combining shapes",
    "introduction": "<p><font color='#1565C0'><b>Combining shapes</b></font> is a <b>2D shape</b>. It is flat, so we can draw it on paper.</p><p>It means joining simple flat shapes to make a new picture or bigger shape. Learning this helps children name shapes in pictures, toys, signs, books, and classrooms. It helps children see how pictures, buildings, patterns, and designs are made.</p>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3><font color='#2E7D32'>Basic idea</font></h3><p>Small <b>2D shapes</b> can join to make larger shapes or pictures.</p><h3><font color='#6A1B9A'>How it works</font></h3><ol><li>Choose simple shapes.</li><li>Place sides close together.</li><li>Notice the new outside shape or picture.</li></ol><h3><font color='#EF6C00'>Why this is true</font></h3><p>When shapes touch, their edges form a new outline. The outline tells us the new shape or picture.</p><h3><font color='#00838F'>Where we use it</font></h3><ul><li>Making a house from a square and triangle</li><li>Creating rangoli designs</li><li>Building pictures with paper pieces</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> Thinking the small shapes disappear.<br><b>Correct idea:</b> The small shapes are still parts of the bigger picture.</p><p><b>Key Point:</b> Combining shapes makes new pictures or larger shapes from smaller shapes.</p>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<ul><li><b>Easy:</b> A square plus a triangle can make a house picture.</li><li><b>Compare:</b> Two triangles can make a square only when their sides fit correctly.</li><li><b>Use:</b> Artists use simple shapes to draw objects.</li></ul>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<p>Combining shapes is like using puzzle pieces. Each piece keeps its shape, but together they make a new picture.</p>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<p>This idea prepares students for <b>composition</b> and <b>decomposition</b>. Composition joins shapes. Decomposition breaks a shape into parts.</p>",
    "practicePrompt": "<p>Use a square and a triangle to draw a house. Name each shape you used and point to the new outside outline.</p>",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 47,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__cube",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Cube",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Cube</h2><p>A cube is a solid shape with six equal square faces.</p>\n<p>It is a 3D shape, so it has length, breadth, and height.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cube</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>cube</strong> is like a box with all sides equal.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>It has 6 square faces, 12 edges, and 8 corners.</p>\n<p>For Class 1, children mainly identify its equal box shape.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Equal square faces make every side look the same size.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Cubes appear in dice, ice cubes, blocks, and some toy boxes.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may call any box a cube.</p>\n<p>A cube needs equal length, breadth, and height.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Cube has square faces.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A cube is a 3D shape with six equal square faces.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cube</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>A <strong>dice</strong> is a cube.</li><li>An <strong>ice cube</strong> is cube-like.</li><li>Building blocks may be cubes.</li><li>A cube is like a square that became a box.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cube becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look for a solid box shape with equal sides.</li><li>If every face is a square, it is a cube.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Later, students learn cube surface area and volume.</p>\n<p>Recognizing faces, edges, and corners is the first step.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find or draw a cube.</li><li>Count its faces, edges, and corners with help.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555368
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 48,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__cuboid",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Cuboid",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>Cuboid explains a specific idea inside 3D Shapes.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Cuboid needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cuboid</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Cuboid, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Cuboid with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cuboid becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look for a box shape.</li><li>If its faces are rectangles and it has length, breadth, and height, it is a cuboid.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cuboid connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find three cuboid objects at home.</li><li>Draw one and write why it is a cuboid.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555368
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 49,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__sphere",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Sphere",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Sphere</h2><p>Sphere explains a specific idea inside 3D Shapes.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Sphere</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>sphere</strong> is round like a ball.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Its surface curves equally in all directions.</p>\n<p>It can roll because it has no flat face.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The curved surface lets it move smoothly.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Spheres appear in balls, marbles, oranges, globes, and some beads.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may call a flat circle a sphere.</p>\n<p>A circle is flat, but a sphere is solid.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Sphere is a ball shape.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A sphere is a round 3D shape with no corners.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Sphere, draw a small labelled figure first.</p>\n<p>Mark the given sides, angles, or units.</p>\n<p>Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Sphere but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Sphere becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Sphere</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Sphere, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Sphere with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Sphere becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Pick up a ball.</li><li>It is not flat.</li><li>It curves all around.</li><li>That solid round shape is called a sphere.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Sphere connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Sphere.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555369
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 50,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__cone",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Cone",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Cone</h2><p>A cone is a solid shape with one flat circular face and one pointed top.</p>\n<p>It is seen in many everyday objects.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cone</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>cone</strong> has a round base and a point.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>The curved surface rises from the circular base to one point.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The circular base lets it stand, and the pointed top gives its cone shape.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Cones appear in ice-cream cones, party hats, traffic cones, and funnels.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may confuse a cone with a cylinder.</p>\n<p>A cylinder has two flat circular faces, but a cone has one point.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Cone has one point.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A cone has one circular face and one pointed top.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cone</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>An <strong>ice-cream cone</strong> is cone-shaped.</li><li>A <strong>party hat</strong> is like a cone.</li><li>A <strong>traffic cone</strong> marks roads.</li><li>A cone is like a circle pulled up to a point.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cone becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look for a round bottom and a pointed top.</li><li>If both are present, the solid shape is a cone.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cones are used later in volume and surface area.</p>\n<p>Children first learn the base, curved face, and vertex.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw a cone.</li><li>Circle its flat base and mark its pointed top.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555369
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 51,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__cylinder",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Cylinder",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>A cylinder is a solid shape with two flat circular faces and one curved surface.</p>\n<p>It is common in bottles, cans, and pipes.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>cylinder</strong> has two equal circles, one at each end.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>The curved surface joins the two circular faces.</p>\n<p>It can roll on its curved side and stand on a flat face.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The circular faces are parallel and equal.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Cylinders appear in water bottles, cans, chalk pieces, pipes, and batteries.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may call a cylinder a circle.</p>\n<p>A circle is flat, but a cylinder is solid.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Cylinder has two circle faces.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A cylinder has two circular faces and one curved surface.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cylinder</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>A <strong>water bottle</strong> is cylinder-like.</li><li>A <strong>can</strong> is a cylinder.</li><li>A <strong>pipe</strong> is often cylindrical.</li><li>It is like a circle stretched into a tube.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cylinder becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look for two round flat ends and a curved side.</li><li>If you see both, it is a cylinder.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cylinders are useful in packaging and pipes.</p>\n<p>Later, students learn their volume and curved surface area.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find a cylindrical object.</li><li>Check whether it can stand and roll.</li><li>Write your observation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555369
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 52,
    "id": "class-1__geometry__3d-shapes__rolling-and-sliding",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "3D Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Rolling and sliding",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Geometry / 3D Shapes</p><h2>Rolling and sliding</h2><p>Rolling and sliding describe how solid shapes move.</p>\n<p>Some shapes roll, some slide, and some can do both.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Rolling and sliding</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Curved surfaces help objects roll.</p>\n<p>Flat faces help objects slide.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>A ball rolls because it has a curved surface.</p>\n<p>A box slides because it has flat faces.</p>\n<p>A cylinder can roll on its curved side and slide on its flat face.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Shape affects movement.</p>\n<p>Curves turn smoothly, while flat faces stay level.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We see wheels, balls, bottles, books, and boxes move.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may think all round-looking objects roll the same way.</p>\n<p>Check the surface.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Curved rolls, flat slides.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A shape’s surface decides how it moves.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Rolling and sliding</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>A <strong>football</strong> rolls.</li><li>A <strong>book</strong> slides.</li><li>A <strong>bottle</strong> can roll or slide.</li><li>It is like comparing a ball and a box on the floor.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Rolling and sliding becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Put objects gently on a table.</li><li>Push them.</li><li>If they turn over and over, they roll.</li><li>If they move flat, they slide.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This idea connects geometry with motion.</p>\n<p>It helps children see that shape properties affect real-world behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Test a cube, sphere, and cylinder.</li><li>Write whether each one rolls, slides, or both.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555370
  },
  {
