    "sourceRow": 191,
    "id": "class-3__fractions__fractions__comparing-simple-fractions",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Fractions",
    "topic": "Fractions",
    "subtopic": "Comparing simple fractions",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Fractions / Fractions</p><h2>Comparing simple fractions</h2><p>Comparing simple fractions explains a specific idea inside Fractions.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Fractions accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Comparing simple fractions</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Fractions compare parts of the same-sized whole.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>If denominators are the same, compare numerators.</p>\n<p>If numerators are the same, the smaller denominator gives the larger piece.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Equal denominators mean equal-sized parts.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it for sharing pizza, cake, chocolate, and lengths.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may compare only bottom numbers without thinking of size.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Same bottom, bigger top is bigger.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Compare fractions by thinking about equal parts.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Comparing simple fractions, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Comparing simple fractions but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Comparing simple fractions becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Comparing simple fractions</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Comparing simple fractions, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Comparing simple fractions with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Comparing simple fractions becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Comparing simple fractions is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Comparing simple fractions connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Comparing simple fractions.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555432
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 192,
    "id": "class-3__fractions__fractions__fractions-on-number-line",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Fractions",
    "topic": "Fractions",
    "subtopic": "Fractions on number line",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Fractions / Fractions</p><h2>Fractions on number line</h2><p>Fractions on number line means placing fractions at correct positions between whole numbers.</p>\n<p>It is useful because it shows fractions as numbers, not only shape parts.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fractions on number line</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A number line can show fractions between 0 and 1.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Divide the space from 0 to 1 into equal parts.</p>\n<p>Count the parts to locate the fraction.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Equal spaces show equal fractional steps.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use number lines for measuring, comparing, time, and later decimals.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may divide the line into unequal spaces.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Equal parts on the line.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Fractions are numbers on a number line.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Fractions on number line, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Fractions on number line but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Fractions on number line becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fractions on number line</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Fractions on number line, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Fractions on number line with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fractions on number line becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fractions on number line is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Fractions on number line connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Fractions on number line.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555432
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 193,
    "id": "class-3__fractions__fractions__fraction-of-a-quantity",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Fractions",
    "topic": "Fractions",
    "subtopic": "Fraction of a quantity",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Fractions / Fractions</p><h2>Fraction of a quantity</h2><p>Fraction of a quantity means finding a fractional part of a group or amount.</p>\n<p>It is useful because it helps share money, objects, and marks.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fraction of a quantity</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A fraction of a quantity means part of a total amount.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Divide the total by the denominator.</p>\n<p>Then multiply by the numerator if needed.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The denominator makes equal groups, and the numerator chooses groups.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it for sharing chocolates, money, class groups, and measurements.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may multiply first when simple sharing is needed.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Divide by bottom, choose by top.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Fraction of a quantity uses equal sharing.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Fraction of a quantity, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Fraction of a quantity but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Fraction of a quantity becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fraction of a quantity</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Fraction of a quantity, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Fraction of a quantity with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fraction of a quantity becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fraction of a quantity is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Fraction of a quantity connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Fraction of a quantity.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555433
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 194,
    "id": "class-3__geometry__basic-geometry__point",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Basic Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Point",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Geometry / Basic Geometry</p><h2>Point</h2><p>Point explains a specific idea inside Basic Geometry.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Point</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>point</strong> has position but no length, breadth, or height.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Mark a tiny dot and name it with a capital letter like A.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Geometry needs exact locations before making lines and shapes.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use points on maps, diagrams, grids, and corners of shapes.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may draw a large dot and think size matters.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Point means position.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A point shows an exact location.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Point, draw a small labelled figure first.</p>\n<p>Mark the given sides, angles, or units.</p>\n<p>Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Point but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Point becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Point</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Point, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Point with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Point becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Point is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Point connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw three points A, B, and C.</li><li>Use them as corners of a triangle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555433
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 195,
    "id": "class-3__geometry__basic-geometry__line",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Basic Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Line",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Geometry / Basic Geometry</p><h2>Line</h2><p>Line explains a specific idea inside Basic Geometry.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Line</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>line</strong> is straight and has no endpoints.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Draw a straight path with arrows on both ends to show it continues.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>A line is an ideal geometry object, not a short drawn segment.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use lines in maps, grids, drawings, roads, and construction plans.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may call any short side a full line.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Line has arrows both ways.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A line extends forever in both directions.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Line, draw a small labelled figure first.</p>\n<p>Mark the given sides, angles, or units.</p>\n<p>Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Line but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Line becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Line</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Line, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Line with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Line becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Line is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Line connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Line.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555434
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 196,
    "id": "class-3__geometry__basic-geometry__line-segment",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Basic Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Line segment",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Geometry / Basic Geometry</p><h2>Line segment</h2><p>Line segment explains a specific idea inside Basic Geometry.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Line segment</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>line segment</strong> has a fixed length.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Mark two endpoints.</p>\n<p>Join them with a straight path.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Endpoints stop the line, so the segment can be measured.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use line segments for shape sides, distance on maps, and measuring objects.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may forget to mark endpoints.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Segment stops.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A line segment has two endpoints.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Line segment, draw a small labelled figure first.</p>\n<p>Mark the given sides, angles, or units.</p>\n<p>Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Line segment but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Line segment becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Line segment</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Line segment, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Line segment with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Line segment becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Line segment is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Line segment connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
