  {
    "sourceRow": 234,
    "id": "class-3__data-and-patterns__patterns__input-output-patterns-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 3",
    "chapter": "Data and Patterns",
    "topic": "Patterns",
    "subtopic": "Input-output patterns introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Data and Patterns / Patterns</p><h2>Input-output patterns introduction</h2><p>Input-output patterns introduction explains a specific idea inside Patterns.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Data and Patterns accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Input-output patterns introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>An <strong>input-output rule</strong> tells how to change each input.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Look at input and output pairs.</p>\n<p>Find the same operation used each time.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>A fixed rule gives exactly related outputs.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use rules in machines, tables, games, prices, and coding.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may use a rule that works once but not for all pairs.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Rule must fit every row.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Input-output patterns use one rule for all pairs.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Input-output patterns introduction, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6.</p>\n<p>Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Input-output patterns introduction but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a small table, bar graph, probability tree, or sample list so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Input-output patterns introduction becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Input-output patterns introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Input-output patterns introduction, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6. Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Input-output patterns introduction with school marks, cricket scores, weather data, survey answers, or game results.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a small table, bar graph, probability tree, or sample list and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Input-output patterns introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Input-output patterns introduction is about reading information from data.</li><li>Start with a small table, compute carefully, then say what the number means.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Input-output patterns introduction connects sample information with uncertainty, modelling, and inference.</p>\n<p>More advanced statistics studies assumptions, variability, bias, and how conclusions change when the data model changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Input-output patterns introduction.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555456
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 235,
    "id": "class-4__number-system__large-numbers__numbers-to-100000",
    "classLevel": "Class 4",
    "chapter": "Number System",
    "topic": "Large Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Numbers to 100000",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 4 / Number System / Large Numbers</p><h2>Numbers to 100000</h2><p>Numbers to 100000 explains a specific idea inside Large Numbers.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Number System accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Numbers to 100000</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Numbers to 100000 use more place values.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Read digits by place value from left to right.</p>\n<p>Use thousands and lakhs correctly.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>The base-ten system groups digits by place.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use large numbers in population, prices, bank accounts, and distances.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may misplace commas or zeros.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Place value first.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Large numbers are read by place value.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Numbers to 100000, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Numbers to 100000 but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Numbers to 100000 becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Numbers to 100000</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Numbers to 100000, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Numbers to 100000 with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Numbers to 100000 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Numbers to 100000 is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Numbers to 100000 connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Numbers to 100000.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555457
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 236,
    "id": "class-4__number-system__large-numbers__indian-place-value-system",
    "classLevel": "Class 4",
    "chapter": "Number System",
    "topic": "Large Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Indian place value system",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 4 / Number System / Large Numbers</p><h2>Indian place value system</h2><p>Indian place value system means reading numbers using ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, lakhs, and crores.</p>\n<p>It is useful because it is used in India for money and population.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Indian place value system</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>The Indian system groups digits as ones, thousands, lakhs, and crores.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Place commas from the right: first after 3 digits, then after every 2 digits.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>This grouping matches Indian number names.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it in Indian prices, bank amounts, population, and news.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may place commas like the international system.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>3, then 2, then 2.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Indian place value uses lakhs and crores.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Indian place value system, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Indian place value system but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Indian place value system becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Indian place value system</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Indian place value system, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Indian place value system with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Indian place value system becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Indian place value system is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Indian place value system connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Indian place value system.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555457
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 237,
    "id": "class-4__number-system__large-numbers__international-place-value-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 4",
    "chapter": "Number System",
    "topic": "Large Numbers",
    "subtopic": "International place value introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 4 / Number System / Large Numbers</p><h2>International place value introduction</h2><p>International place value introduction means reading numbers using ones, thousands, millions, and billions.</p>\n<p>It is useful because it helps read global data and international books.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>International place value introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>The international system groups digits in threes.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>From the right, put commas after every 3 digits.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Groups of three match thousand, million, and billion names.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it in world population, science data, money, and international reports.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may mix lakh with million in the same reading.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Groups of three.</p>\n<p>Key Point: International place value uses thousands and millions.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For International place value introduction, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of International place value introduction but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: International place value introduction becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>International place value introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For International place value introduction, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use International place value introduction with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>International place value introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>International place value introduction is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>International place value introduction connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for International place value introduction.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555458
