    "sourceRow": 276,
    "id": "class-4__geometry__angles-and-shapes__rectangle-properties",
    "classLevel": "Class 4",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Angles and Shapes",
    "subtopic": "Rectangle properties",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 4 / Geometry / Angles and Shapes</p><h2>Rectangle properties</h2><p>Rectangle properties explains a specific idea inside Angles and Shapes.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Rectangle properties</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>rectangle</strong> has four right angles and opposite sides equal.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Check four sides, four right angles, and equal opposite sides.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>These properties define a rectangle.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>mobile screens, doors, notebooks, boards, and rooms</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may think a turned rectangle is not a rectangle.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Opposite sides match.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A rectangle has four right angles and equal opposite sides.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Rectangle properties, draw a small labelled figure first.</p>\n<p>Mark the given sides, angles, or units.</p>\n<p>Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Rectangle properties but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Rectangle properties becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Rectangle properties</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Rectangle properties, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Rectangle properties with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Rectangle properties becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Rectangle properties is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Rectangle properties connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Rectangle properties.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555477
