  {
    "sourceRow": 634,
    "id": "class-9__geometry__quadrilaterals__parallelogram-theorems",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Parallelogram theorems",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Geometry / Quadrilaterals</p><h2>Parallelogram theorems</h2><p>A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel.</p>\n<p>Its theorems describe equal sides, equal angles, and diagonals.</p>\n<p>They help prove a shape is a parallelogram and find missing lengths.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Parallelogram theorems</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Parallelograms have equal opposite parts and bisecting diagonals.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Parallelogram theorems</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A slanting tile pattern often forms parallelograms.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides but is not usually a parallelogram.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Parallelogram frames appear in scissor lifts and drafting tools.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Draw both diagonals. Notice each diagonal cuts the other into two equal parts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Parallelogram theorems becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A parallelogram is a pushed rectangle; opposite parts still match.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>In vector geometry, ABCD is a parallelogram exactly when A + C = B + D for position vectors.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>In parallelogram ABCD, angle A = 70 degrees.</li><li>Find angle C and explain.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932429
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 635,
    "id": "class-9__geometry__quadrilaterals__midpoint-theorem-applications",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Midpoint theorem applications",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Geometry / Quadrilaterals</p><h2>Midpoint theorem applications</h2><p>Midpoint theorem applications use triangle midpoint facts inside larger figures.</p>\n<p>They often prove parallel sides or find half-lengths.</p>\n<p>They simplify quadrilateral proofs by creating useful triangles.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Midpoint theorem applications</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Midpoint theorem applications work by finding the right triangle inside the figure.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Midpoint theorem applications</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Joining midpoints in a paper triangle creates a smaller matching triangle.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Joining random points on sides does not guarantee a parallel half-length segment.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Map scaling and frame design use midpoint segments.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Draw a diagonal to split the quadrilateral into triangles, then mark midpoints.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Midpoint theorem applications becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Break the bigger shape into triangles.</li><li>Midpoints then reveal parallel mini-sides.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The theorem is an affine result, so it remains true under shearing transformations that preserve midpoints and parallelism.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw quadrilateral ABCD with diagonal AC.</li><li>Mark midpoints on AB and BC.</li><li>What theorem can you use?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932430
  },
