  {
    "sourceRow": 644,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__heron-formula",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Heron formula",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Heron formula</h2><p>Heron&#x27;s formula finds a triangle&#x27;s area when all three sides are known.</p>\n<p>It avoids needing a height.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Heron formula</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Area = sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)).</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Heron formula</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For sides 3,4,5, s=6, area=sqrt(6x3x2x1)=6 square units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check triangle inequality before calculating area.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Surveying, land plots, and triangle measurements use it.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine the height hidden inside the three side lengths.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Heron formula becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Three sides can give area without height.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Heron&#x27;s formula is equivalent to the determinant-based area formula after algebraic simplification.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find the area of a triangle with sides 5, 5, and 6.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 645,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__area-triangle",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Area triangle",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Area triangle</h2><p>Triangle area measures the surface inside a triangle.</p>\n<p>It uses a base and the perpendicular height.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Area triangle</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Area = 1/2 x base x height.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Area triangle</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If base=10 cm and height=6 cm, area=30 square cm.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Fields, roofs, and design patterns use triangle area.</li><li>Visual Understanding: See two copies forming one parallelogram.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Area triangle becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Triangle area is half a matching parallelogram.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The formula works for any chosen base with its corresponding altitude.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A triangle has base 12 m and height 7 m.</li><li>Find its area.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 646,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__area-quadrilateral",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Area quadrilateral",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Area quadrilateral</h2><p>A quadrilateral&#x27;s area can often be found by splitting it into triangles.</p>\n<p>This makes an irregular four-sided shape manageable.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Area quadrilateral</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Area of quadrilateral = sum of areas of non-overlapping parts.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Area quadrilateral</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A quadrilateral split into triangles of areas 12 and 15 has total area 27 square units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Side lengths around the edge give perimeter, not area.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Land areas, floor plans, and maps use this method.</li><li>Visual Understanding: A diagonal turns four sides into two triangles.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Area quadrilateral becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cut the shape into triangles.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>General quadrilateral area can also be expressed using diagonals and included angle.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Split a quadrilateral into two triangles with areas 18 and 22.</li><li>Find total area.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 647,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cube",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cube",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Cube</h2><p>A cube is a solid with six equal square faces.</p>\n<p>All edges have the same length.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cube</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Surface area = 6a^2; Volume = a^3.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cube</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If a=4 cm, surface area=96 square cm and volume=64 cubic cm.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: For a cube, l=b=h=a.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Dice, boxes, and blocks model cubes.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture six identical square faces.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cube becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A cube is a perfectly equal box.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>A cube is a regular hexahedron and one of the Platonic solids.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find surface area and volume of a cube of side 7 cm.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 648,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cuboid",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cuboid",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>A cuboid is a box-shaped solid with rectangular faces.</p>\n<p>Length, breadth, and height may differ.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>TSA = 2(lb+bh+hl); Volume = lbh.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cuboid</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For l=5,b=3,h=2, volume=30 cubic units and TSA=62 square units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Surface area uses square units; volume uses cubic units.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Rooms, books, tanks, and packages are cuboids.</li><li>Visual Understanding: See layers of rectangles stacked upward.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cuboid becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cuboid is a rectangular box.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cuboids are rectangular prisms in three-dimensional Euclidean geometry.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A box is 8 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm.</li><li>Find volume.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 649,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cylinder",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cylinder",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>A cylinder has two circular bases and one curved surface.</p>\n<p>Its size depends on radius and height.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Volume = pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = 2 pi r h.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cylinder</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If r=3 and h=10, volume=90pi cubic units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Radius is half the diameter.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Cans, pipes, and water tanks use cylinder formulas.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Unroll the side into a rectangle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cylinder becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cylinder is a stack of equal circles.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>A right circular cylinder is a prism-like solid with circular cross-sections.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find volume of a cylinder with radius 4 cm and height 6 cm.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 650,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cone",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cone",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Cone</h2><p>A cone has one circular base and one pointed vertex.</p>\n<p>Its volume is one-third of a matching cylinder.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cone</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Volume = 1/3 pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = pi r l.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cone</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If r=3,h=4, volume=12pi cubic units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Volume uses vertical height, not slant height.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Ice-cream cones, funnels, and traffic cones use cone formulas.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine shrinking circular slices up to a point.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cone becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cone volume is one-third cylinder volume.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cone formulas follow from similarity or integration of circular cross-sections.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A cone has r=5 cm and h=12 cm.</li><li>Find volume.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 651,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__sphere",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Sphere",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Sphere</h2><p>A sphere is a perfectly round solid where every surface point is the same distance from the centre.</p>\n<p>That distance is the radius.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Sphere</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Surface area = 4 pi r^2; Volume = 4/3 pi r^3.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Sphere</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If r=3, surface area=36pi and volume=36pi cubic units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If diameter is given, divide by 2 first.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Balls, globes, and bubbles are sphere models.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Every radius reaches the surface equally.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Sphere becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Sphere is a 3D circle-like solid.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Sphere volume can be derived using Cavalieri&#x27;s principle or integration.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find surface area of a sphere with diameter 10 cm.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 652,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__hemisphere",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Hemisphere",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Hemisphere</h2><p>A hemisphere is half of a sphere.</p>\n<p>It has one curved surface and one circular flat face.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Hemisphere</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>CSA = 2 pi r^2; TSA = 3 pi r^2; Volume = 2/3 pi r^3.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Hemisphere</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If r=3, curved area=18pi, total area=27pi, volume=18pi.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: CSA excludes the base; TSA includes it.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Bowls, domes, and half-globes use hemisphere formulas.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Visualize a sphere sliced through its centre.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Hemisphere becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Hemisphere is half a ball plus a circular base.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Hemisphere measures follow directly from sphere formulas and disk area.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find CSA and TSA for a hemisphere of radius 7 cm.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932431
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 653,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__composite-solids-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Composite solids introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Mensuration / Heron and Solids</p><h2>Composite solids introduction</h2><p>Composite solids are made by joining or cutting simpler solids.</p>\n<p>Their measurements come from adding or subtracting known parts.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Composite solids introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Composite volume = sum of included part volumes - removed part volumes.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Composite solids introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A cylinder of volume 100 with a cone of volume 30 on top has total volume 130.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Internal joined faces are not part of outside surface area.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Tanks, toys, buildings, and containers use composite solids.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Color only the outside surface to avoid hidden faces.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Composite solids introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Break the solid into known pieces.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Composite-solid work uses decomposition, often extended by integration for curved or irregular solids.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A cuboid has a cylindrical hole removed.</li><li>Should you add or subtract the cylinder volume?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
