  {
    "sourceRow": 661,
    "id": "class-9__statistics-and-probability__probability__empirical-probability",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Empirical probability",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 9 / Statistics and Probability / Probability</p><h2>Empirical probability</h2><p>Empirical probability is based on results from experiments.</p>\n<p>It estimates chance using observed data.</p>\n<p>It helps understand games, surveys, weather estimates, and random experiments.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Empirical probability</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Empirical probability uses actual experiment results, not only expected outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Empirical probability</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Tossing a coin ten times gives observed heads and tails.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Getting 7 heads in 10 tosses does not prove the coin always gives heads.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: A shop may estimate demand by observing previous customer choices.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Make a tally table. The fraction of tallies in one row gives the estimate.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Empirical probability becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Experimental chance means &#x27;what happened in our trials.&#x27;</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Empirical probability is relative frequency.</p>\n<p>The law of large numbers explains why it often approaches theoretical probability as trials increase.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A die is rolled 40 times and 6 appears 5 times.</li><li>Find the empirical probability of getting 6.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932433
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 662,
