  {
    "sourceRow": 664,
    "id": "class-10__number-systems__real-numbers__fundamental-theorem-of-arithmetic",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Number Systems",
    "topic": "Real Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 10 / Number Systems / Real Numbers</p><h2>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</h2><p>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic is a specific idea in Real Numbers.</p>\n<p>The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 60=2^2 x 3 x 5. Any correct prime factorization of 60 uses the same primes with the same powers.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Number Systems, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic is about one main check: The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order.</li><li>Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The theorem depends on Euclid&#x27;s lemma.</p>\n<p>It is the reason gcd, lcm, and divisibility tests work prime by prime.</p>\n<p>This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Real Numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932433
  },
