  {
    "sourceRow": 714,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__standard-angle-values",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Standard-angle values",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 10 / Trigonometry / Trigonometric Ratios</p><h2>Standard-angle values</h2><p>Standard-angle values are exact trigonometric ratios for common angles.</p>\n<p>They help solve problems quickly without a calculator.</p>\n<p>They are used in equations, heights, distances, and identities.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Standard-angle values</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Standard-angle values are exact ratios from special triangles.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Standard-angle values</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A square cut diagonally gives a 45-45-90 triangle.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: sin30 degrees = 1/2, but sin60 degrees = sqrt3/2.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Standard values simplify many Class 10 trigonometry problems.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Draw the two special triangles and write their side ratios.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Standard-angle values becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Memorise the values by understanding the two special triangles.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Standard values also appear as coordinates on the unit circle at special angles.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find sin60 degrees, cos45 degrees, and tan30 degrees using special triangles.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932441
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 715,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__relationships",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Relationships",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 10 / Trigonometry / Trigonometric Ratios</p><h2>Relationships</h2><p>Trigonometric relationships connect the six ratios.</p>\n<p>They help convert one ratio into another.</p>\n<p>They reduce work in identities and equation solving.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Relationships</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Trigonometric relationships come from comparing the same triangle sides.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Relationships</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If a fraction is 3/5, its reciprocal is 5/3.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: sin theta + cos theta is not generally equal to 1.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Relationships help simplify trigonometric expressions.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Write all ratios from one labelled right triangle and compare them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Relationships becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Most trig ratios are built from sine and cosine.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Quotient and reciprocal identities remain valid wherever denominators are non-zero in the unit-circle definition.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>If tan theta = 5/12, find cot theta and explain.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932441
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 716,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__identities",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Identities",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 10 / Trigonometry / Trigonometric Ratios</p><h2>Identities</h2><p>Trigonometric identities are equations true for all allowed angle values.</p>\n<p>They are not solved like ordinary equations.</p>\n<p>They help simplify expressions and prove trigonometric results.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Identities</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Trigonometric identities are always-true relationships between trigonometric ratios.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Identities</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A 3-4-5 triangle gives (3/5)^2 + (4/5)^2 = 1.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: sin theta + cos theta = 1 is false for theta = 45 degrees.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Identities simplify equations in physics, waves, and geometry.</li><li>Visual Understanding: On the unit circle, x = cos theta and y = sin theta, so x^2 + y^2 = 1.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Identities becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>An identity is always true when the expressions are defined.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Pythagorean identities arise from the unit circle equation x^2+y^2=1 and quotient identities follow by division.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Prove 1 + tan^2 theta = sec^2 theta by dividing sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta = 1 by cos^2 theta.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272932441
  },
  {
