    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 10 / Mensuration / Circles and Solids</p><h2>Circumference</h2><p>Circumference is a specific idea in Circles and Solids.</p>\n<p>Circumference is a shape or measurement idea.</p>\n<p>A labelled diagram shows which lengths, angles, or symmetries control the answer.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Circumference</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Circumference is a shape or measurement idea.</p>\n<p>A labelled diagram shows which lengths, angles, or symmetries control the answer.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Circumference</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Circumference, draw a labelled figure and mark the known lengths, angles, or symmetry line. Then apply the shape rule and check the unit.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Circumference may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Mensuration, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Circumference.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Circumference becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Circumference is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Circumference becomes deeper when examples, counterexamples, and hypotheses are studied together rather than as a memorized rule.</p></section></div>",
