    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For 3D distance, use a circle, sphere, torus, or simple surface. Identify the local object and the global invariant being studied.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, 3D distance may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Coordinate Geometry, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in 3D distance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>3D distance becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
