  {
    "sourceRow": 983,
    "id": "school-enrichment-olympiad__number-theory__divisibility-and-congruences__fermat-s-little-theorem",
    "classLevel": "School Enrichment / Olympiad",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Divisibility and Congruences",
    "subtopic": "Fermat's Little Theorem",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">School Enrichment / Olympiad / Number Theory / Divisibility and Congruences</p><h2>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</h2><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem is a standard result about powers modulo a prime.</p>\n<p>It says that prime moduli create a clean repeating structure for nonzero residues.</p>\n<p>This is useful in Olympiad number theory, modular arithmetic, and cryptography.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem needs a prime modulus and a base not divisible by that prime.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 2^4=16 is congruent to 1 modulo 5.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Modulo 6 is not prime, so Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem does not apply directly.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: To simplify 3^100 modulo 7, reduce the exponent using cycles of length 6.</li><li>Visual Model: Make a table of powers and remainders to see the repeating cycle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Use Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem only with a prime modulus.</li><li>Check the prime first, then check that the base is not a multiple of that prime.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This theorem is Lagrange&#x27;s theorem applied to the multiplicative group of nonzero residues modulo p.</p>\n<p>It is a special prime-modulus case of Euler&#x27;s theorem.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem to simplify 2^20 modulo 5.</li><li>State why the theorem applies before calculating.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273375974
  },
