  {
    "sourceRow": 1084,
    "id": "ug-year-1__calculus-i__integration__fundamental-theorem-of-calculus",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Calculus I",
    "topic": "Integration",
    "subtopic": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 1 / Calculus I / Integration</p><h2>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</h2><p>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus is a specific idea in Integration.</p>\n<p>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: If F(x)=x^3/3, then integral from 0 to 2 of x^2 dx is F(2)-F(0)=8/3.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fundamental Theorem of Calculus may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Calculus I, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus is about one main check: The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes.</li><li>Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Part I differentiates an accumulation function.</p>\n<p>Part II evaluates definite integrals using antiderivatives under continuity assumptions.</p>\n<p>This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Integration.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273375989
  },
