  {
    "sourceRow": 1145,
    "id": "ug-year-2__calculus-ii__sequences-and-series__radius-of-convergence",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 2",
    "chapter": "Calculus II",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Radius of convergence",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 2 / Calculus II / Sequences and Series</p><h2>Radius of convergence</h2><p>Radius of convergence explains a specific idea inside Sequences and Series.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Calculus II accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Radius of convergence</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The radius of convergence is the distance from the center within which a power series converges.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Radius of convergence</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: sum x^n converges for |x|&lt;1, so radius is 1.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: State the hypotheses first, then apply the theorem or method.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in calculus, modelling, physics, approximation, and rigorous proof.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use a graph, sequence plot, shrinking interval, or accumulated area to see the behavior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Radius of convergence becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Radius of convergence studies change, closeness, or accumulated value.</li><li>A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Radius of convergence connects local change, limiting behavior, approximation, or accumulation.</p>\n<p>Advanced analysis makes this precise using functions, convergence, continuity, differentiability, or integration conditions.</p></section></div>",
