  {
    "sourceRow": 1179,
    "id": "ug-year-2__linear-algebra-ii__advanced-linear-algebra__eigenvalues",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 2",
    "chapter": "Linear Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Linear Algebra",
    "subtopic": "Eigenvalues",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 2 / Linear Algebra II / Advanced Linear Algebra</p><h2>Eigenvalues</h2><p>Eigenvalues explains a specific idea inside Advanced Linear Algebra.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Linear Algebra II accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Eigenvalues</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Eigenvalues is controlled by the structure of the linear map or inner product.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Eigenvalues</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For A=diag(2,3), eigenvalues are 2 and 3.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check matrix size, field, inner product, and required assumptions first.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in differential equations, data science, optimization, geometry, and quantum mechanics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use arrows, perpendicular projections, eigendirections, or quadratic surfaces.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Eigenvalues becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Eigenvalues is like keeping a balance.</li><li>Each step must preserve the same value or relationship, so the final answer still fits the original problem.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Eigenvalues connects symbols with structure-preserving operations.</p>\n<p>In higher algebra, the same reasoning appears through equations, functions, matrices, rings, vector spaces, or abstract maps.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Eigenvalues.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273376002
  },
