    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: (2x+y)dx + x dy=0 is exact.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Classify the ODE first, then choose the matching method and verify by substitution.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in mechanics, population models, circuits, heat, and control systems.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use a slope field, phase plane, or solution curve to see behavior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Exact equations becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Exact equations is like keeping a balance.</li><li>Each step must preserve the same value or relationship, so the final answer still fits the original problem.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Exact equations connects symbols with structure-preserving operations.</p>\n<p>In higher algebra, the same reasoning appears through equations, functions, matrices, rings, vector spaces, or abstract maps.</p></section></div>",
