    "subtopic": "Cauchy sequences",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 2 / Real Analysis I / Real Numbers and Sequences</p><h2>Cauchy sequences</h2><p>Cauchy sequences explains a specific idea inside Real Numbers and Sequences.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Real Analysis I accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cauchy sequences</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A sequence is Cauchy if its terms eventually become arbitrarily close to each other.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cauchy sequences</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 1/n is Cauchy because far-out terms are close to 0 and each other.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: State the hypotheses first, then apply the theorem or method.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in calculus, modelling, physics, approximation, and rigorous proof.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use a graph, sequence plot, shrinking interval, or accumulated area to see the behavior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cauchy sequences becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cauchy sequences studies change, closeness, or accumulated value.</li><li>A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cauchy sequences connects local change, limiting behavior, approximation, or accumulation.</p>\n<p>Advanced analysis makes this precise using functions, convergence, continuity, differentiability, or integration conditions.</p></section></div>",
