    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Bernoulli data, MLE of p is sample proportion of successes.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: State the random variables, sample method, and assumptions before calculating.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in experiments, surveys, quality control, medicine, business, and machine learning.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use tables, density curves, scatter plots, or sampling diagrams to see the idea.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Maximum likelihood becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Maximum likelihood is about reading information from data.</li><li>Start with a small table, compute carefully, then say what the number means.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Maximum likelihood connects sample information with uncertainty, modelling, and inference.</p>\n<p>More advanced statistics studies assumptions, variability, bias, and how conclusions change when the data model changes.</p></section></div>",
