    "id": "ug-year-3__real-analysis-ii__sequences-of-functions__power-series-rigorous-treatment",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Real Analysis II",
    "topic": "Sequences of Functions",
    "subtopic": "Power series rigorous treatment",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Real Analysis II / Sequences of Functions</p><h2>Power series rigorous treatment</h2><p>Power series rigorous treatment explains a specific idea inside Sequences of Functions.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Real Analysis II accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Power series rigorous treatment</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Power series rigorous treatment depends on the type of convergence and theorem hypotheses.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Power series rigorous treatment</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: sum x^n converges uniformly on [-1/2,1/2].</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Use uniform convergence or a theorem with the correct hypotheses before interchanging limits.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, approximation, power series, differential equations, and numerical methods.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine a whole graph moving toward another graph, then check whether all points get close together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Power series rigorous treatment becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Power series rigorous treatment studies change, closeness, or accumulated value.</li><li>A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Power series rigorous treatment connects local change, limiting behavior, approximation, or accumulation.</p>\n<p>Advanced analysis makes this precise using functions, convergence, continuity, differentiability, or integration conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Power series rigorous treatment.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
