  {
    "sourceRow": 1275,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__complex-plane",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Complex plane",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Complex plane</h2><p>Complex plane studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Complex plane</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The complex plane represents z=a+bi as the point (a,b).</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Complex plane</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: z=3-2i is plotted at (3,-2).</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Complex plane becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>The complex plane is the coordinate plane for complex numbers.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>It identifies C with R^2 while multiplication adds rotation and scaling.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Plot -1+4i and state its real and imaginary parts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909814
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1276,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__complex-differentiability",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Complex differentiability",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Complex differentiability</h2><p>Complex differentiability studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Complex differentiability</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A complex function is differentiable at z0 if the difference quotient has the same limit from every complex direction.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Complex differentiability</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^2 has derivative 2z because [(z+h)^2-z^2]/h=2z+h.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Complex differentiability becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Complex differentiability is stricter than real differentiability.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Complex differentiability on a region leads to analyticity under standard conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use the definition to find derivative of f(z)=z^2 at z=1.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909814
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1277,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__cauchy-riemann-equations",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy-Riemann equations",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Cauchy-Riemann equations</h2><p>Cauchy-Riemann equations studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cauchy-Riemann equations</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>For f=u+iv, the Cauchy-Riemann equations are u_x=v_y and u_y=-v_x, with needed differentiability conditions.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cauchy-Riemann equations</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z^2, u=x^2-y^2 and v=2xy; u_x=2x=v_y and u_y=-2y=-v_x.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cauchy-Riemann equations becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>They test whether real and imaginary parts fit complex differentiability.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>They are necessary, and with suitable smoothness sufficient, for analyticity.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Check Cauchy-Riemann equations for f(z)=conjugate z.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909814
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1278,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__analytic-functions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Analytic functions",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Analytic functions</h2><p>Analytic functions studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Analytic functions</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A function is analytic on a region if it is complex differentiable at every point of that region.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Analytic functions</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^3 is analytic on C.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Analytic functions becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Analytic means differentiable throughout an open region.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Analytic functions have power-series expansions locally.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Give an analytic function and a non-analytic function on C.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909814
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1279,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__elementary-complex-functions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Elementary complex functions",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Elementary complex functions</h2><p>Elementary complex functions studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Elementary complex functions</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Elementary complex functions extend polynomials, exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions to complex inputs.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Elementary complex functions</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: e^(i pi)=-1 is a complex exponential identity.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Elementary complex functions becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>They extend familiar functions into the complex plane.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Branches matter for multi-valued functions such as logarithm and powers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Explain why complex logarithm needs a branch choice.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909814
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1280,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__conformal-maps-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Conformal maps introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Functions</p><h2>Conformal maps introduction</h2><p>Conformal maps introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Conformal maps introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A conformal map preserves angles locally where it is analytic and has non-zero derivative.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Conformal maps introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^2 is conformal away from z=0.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Conformal maps introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Conformal maps bend shapes while keeping tiny angles.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>They connect complex analysis with geometry and harmonic functions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Why is f(z)=z^2 not conformal at z=0?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1281,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__contours",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Contours",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Contours</h2><p>Contours studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Contours</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A contour is a directed curve used as a path for complex integration.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Contours</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: The unit circle contour can be written z(t)=e^(it), 0&lt;=t&lt;=2pi.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Contours becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A contour is a road for integrating complex functions.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Contour choice controls residues, winding, and integral values.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Parametrize the circle |z|=2 counterclockwise.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1282,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__cauchy-s-integral-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy's Integral Theorem",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem</h2><p>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem is a specific idea in Complex Integration.</p>\n<p>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z on the unit circle, the contour integral is zero because f is entire.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Complex Analysis, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem is about one main check: Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero.</li><li>Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The theorem depends on holomorphicity on a region containing the contour and its interior.</p>\n<p>It leads to Cauchy&#x27;s integral formula and residue theory.</p>\n<p>This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Complex Integration.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Cauchy&#x27;s Integral Theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1283,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__cauchy-integral-formula",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy integral formula",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Cauchy integral formula</h2><p>Cauchy integral formula studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cauchy integral formula</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>If f is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour C, then f(a)=1/(2pi i) integral_C f(z)/(z-a) dz.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cauchy integral formula</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Integral of 1/(z-0) around unit circle equals 2pi i.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cauchy integral formula becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A boundary integral can recover an inside value.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The formula implies analytic functions are infinitely differentiable.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use Cauchy integral formula to evaluate integral_C 1/(z-2) dz when 2 is inside C.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1284,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__taylor-series",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Taylor series",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Taylor series</h2><p>Taylor series studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Taylor series</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>An analytic function can be represented locally by a power series about a point.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Taylor series</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: e^z=1+z+z^2/2!+... around 0.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Taylor series becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Taylor series is a local polynomial-like expansion.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>In complex analysis, analyticity is equivalent to local power-series representation.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write the first four terms of sin z about 0.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1285,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__laurent-series",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Laurent series",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Laurent series</h2><p>Laurent series studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Laurent series</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A Laurent series allows negative powers and represents functions near isolated singularities.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Laurent series</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 1/(z(z-1)) has negative powers around z=0 in a punctured region.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Laurent series becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Laurent series is Taylor series with possible pole terms.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The coefficient of (z-a)^(-1) is the residue.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find the Laurent series idea for 1/z near 0.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1286,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__zeros-and-poles",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Zeros and poles",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Zeros and poles</h2><p>Zeros and poles studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Zeros and poles</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A zero is where f(a)=0; a pole is an isolated singularity where f grows like a negative power.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Zeros and poles</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=(z-2)^3 has a zero of order 3 at z=2.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Zeros and poles becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Zeros are roots; poles are controlled blow-ups.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Orders of zeros and poles drive residue and argument-principle calculations.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find the order of the pole of 1/(z-1)^4.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1287,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__residue-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Residue theorem",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Residue theorem</h2><p>Residue theorem studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Residue theorem</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>For isolated singularities inside C, integral_C f(z) dz = 2pi i times the sum of residues.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Residue theorem</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Integral of 1/z around unit circle is 2pi i.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Residue theorem becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Residues are the pieces that survive contour integration.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Residue methods evaluate real integrals and count zeros.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use residue theorem for integral of 3/z around the unit circle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1288,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__argument-principle-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Argument principle introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Argument principle introduction</h2><p>Argument principle introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Argument principle introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The argument principle relates change in argument around a contour to zeros minus poles inside.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Argument principle introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z^2 around unit circle, winding doubles, so zeros counted with multiplicity are 2.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Argument principle introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Watch how f(C) winds around 0.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>It is a bridge between complex integration and root counting.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Explain what zeros minus poles means in the argument principle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909815
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1289,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__rouche-theorem-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Rouche theorem introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Complex Analysis / Complex Integration</p><h2>Rouche theorem introduction</h2><p>Rouche theorem introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals.</p>\n<p>It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Rouche theorem introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>If |g|&lt;|f| on a contour, then f and f+g have the same number of zeros inside.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Rouche theorem introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: z^3 and z^3+0.1 have the same number of zeros inside a large enough unit contour when |0.1|&lt;|z^3| on it.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Rouche theorem introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A smaller boundary disturbance does not change the zero count.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Rouche theorem is a powerful root-counting tool.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use Rouche idea to compare z^4 and z^4+1/2 on |z|=1.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1290,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__group-actions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Group actions",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Group actions</h2><p>Group actions studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Group actions</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A group action assigns each group element a bijection of a set, compatibly with the group operation.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Group actions</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Rotations of a square act on its four vertices.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Group actions becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A group action is a group moving a set.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Actions convert group structure into symmetry of objects.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Give an action of Z on integers by n.k=k+n.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1291,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__orbit-stabilizer",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Orbit-stabilizer",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Orbit-stabilizer</h2><p>Orbit-stabilizer studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Orbit-stabilizer</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>For a finite group action, |G| = |Orbit(x)| |Stabilizer(x)|.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Orbit-stabilizer</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A square&#x27;s rotation group has 4 elements; a vertex orbit has 4 points and stabilizer size 1.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Orbit-stabilizer becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Orbit counts where a point can go; stabilizer counts what fixes it.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Orbit-stabilizer organizes counting in group actions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find orbit and stabilizer of a vertex under rotations of a triangle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1292,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__class-equation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Class equation",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Class equation</h2><p>Class equation studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Class equation</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The class equation partitions a finite group into conjugacy classes, separating central elements.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Class equation</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: In an abelian group, every conjugacy class has one element.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Class equation becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Class equation counts elements by conjugacy behavior.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>It is used to prove results about p-groups and centers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Why do abelian groups have singleton conjugacy classes?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1293,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__sylow-theorems",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Sylow theorems",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Sylow theorems</h2><p>Sylow theorems studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Sylow theorems</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Sylow theorems describe subgroups of order p^k inside finite groups.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Sylow theorems</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A group of order 12 has Sylow 3-subgroups of order 3.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Sylow theorems becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Sylow subgroups capture prime-power parts of a group.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>They are central tools for classifying finite groups.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>State possible numbers of Sylow 3-subgroups in a group of order 12.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1294,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__direct-products",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Direct products",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Direct products</h2><p>Direct products studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Direct products</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>The direct product G x H combines two groups component by component.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Direct products</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z2 x Z3 has operation (a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d) modulo each part.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Direct products becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Direct product runs two groups side by side.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Direct products build larger groups from smaller factors.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>List all elements of Z2 x Z2.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1295,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__finite-abelian-groups-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Finite abelian groups introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Advanced Groups</p><h2>Finite abelian groups introduction</h2><p>Finite abelian groups introduction studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Finite abelian groups introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Every finite abelian group decomposes into a product of cyclic groups of prime-power order.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Finite abelian groups introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z6 is isomorphic to Z2 x Z3 because 2 and 3 are coprime.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Finite abelian groups introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Finite abelian groups are made from cyclic building blocks.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This is a classification theorem for finite abelian groups.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Explain why Z4 and Z2 x Z2 are not isomorphic.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1296,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Ideals",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Ideals</h2><p>Ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Ideals</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>An ideal is an additive subgroup that absorbs multiplication by ring elements.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Ideals</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 2Z is an ideal of Z.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Ideals becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>An ideal is a ring subset closed under outside multiplication.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Ideals are kernels of ring homomorphisms and define quotients.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Show that 5Z is an ideal of Z.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1297,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__quotient-rings",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Quotient rings",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Quotient rings</h2><p>Quotient rings studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Quotient rings</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A quotient ring R/I is made from cosets of an ideal I, with operations defined by cosets.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Quotient rings</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z/5Z has residue classes 0,1,2,3,4.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Quotient rings becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A quotient ring treats ideal elements as zero.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Quotient rings are fundamental in algebraic geometry and number theory.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>In Z/6Z, explain why [2]+[5]=[1] and why answers depend only on remainders.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1298,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__prime-ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Prime ideals",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Prime ideals</h2><p>Prime ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Prime ideals</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A proper ideal P is prime if ab in P implies a in P or b in P.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Prime ideals</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: In Z, 5Z is prime because 5 prime divides ab only when it divides a or b.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Prime ideals becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Prime ideals generalize prime numbers.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>R/P is an integral domain exactly when P is prime.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Is 6Z a prime ideal of Z?</li><li>Give a counterexample.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1299,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__maximal-ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Maximal ideals",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Maximal ideals</h2><p>Maximal ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Maximal ideals</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A proper ideal M is maximal if no proper ideal lies strictly between M and the whole ring.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Maximal ideals</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: In Z, pZ is maximal when p is prime.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Maximal ideals becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Maximal ideals are largest proper ideals.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>R/M is a field exactly when M is maximal in a commutative ring with 1.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>In Z, why is (5) maximal but (6) not maximal?</li><li>Explain using quotient rings.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909816
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1300,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__euclidean-domains",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Euclidean domains",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Euclidean domains</h2><p>Euclidean domains studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Euclidean domains</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A Euclidean domain has a division algorithm controlled by a size function.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Euclidean domains</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z is Euclidean using absolute value.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Euclidean domains becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Euclidean domains allow gcd computations like integers.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Every Euclidean domain is a PID and a UFD.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use Euclidean algorithm in Z to find gcd(30,12).</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909817
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1301,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__pids",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "PIDs",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>PIDs</h2><p>PIDs studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>PIDs</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A principal ideal domain is an integral domain where every ideal is generated by one element.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>PIDs</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z is a PID because every ideal is nZ.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>PIDs becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>PID means every ideal has one generator.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>PIDs connect ideal theory with factorization.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Give the generator of the ideal 12Z in Z.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909817
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1302,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__ufds",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "UFDs",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>UFDs</h2><p>UFDs studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>UFDs</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A unique factorization domain has factorization into irreducibles uniquely up to units and order.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>UFDs</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Z is a UFD by the fundamental theorem of arithmetic.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>UFDs becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>UFD means primes factor numbers reliably.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Every PID is a UFD, but not every UFD is a PID.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Factor 180 into primes and state the uniqueness idea.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909817
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1303,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__polynomial-factorisation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Polynomial factorisation",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Polynomial factorisation</h2><p>Polynomial factorisation studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Polynomial factorisation</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Polynomial factorisation writes a polynomial as a product of simpler polynomials over a chosen field or ring.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Polynomial factorisation</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1) over R.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Polynomial factorisation becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Factorisation depends on the coefficient field.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Irreducibility changes when the base field changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Factor x^2+1 over R and over C.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909817
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1304,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__field-extensions-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Field extensions introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 3 / Abstract Algebra II / Rings and Fields</p><h2>Field extensions introduction</h2><p>Field extensions introduction studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms.</p>\n<p>It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Field extensions introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A field extension K/F is a larger field K containing a smaller field F.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Field extensions introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: C is a field extension of R.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Field extensions introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Extensions add new numbers while keeping field rules.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Field extensions organize roots of polynomials and Galois theory.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Explain why Q(sqrt(2)) is an extension of Q.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909817
  },
  {
