    "subtopic": "Parseval identity",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 4 / Honours / Functional Analysis I / Hilbert Spaces</p><h2>Parseval identity</h2><p>Parseval identity is a specific idea in Hilbert Spaces.</p>\n<p>Parseval identity studies functions, limits, operators, or integrals using precise analytic conditions.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis controls convergence, boundedness, or equality.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Parseval identity</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Parseval identity studies functions, limits, operators, or integrals using precise analytic conditions.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis controls convergence, boundedness, or equality.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Parseval identity</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Parseval identity, test the idea on f(x)=x^2, e^x, or a simple interval. Check the analytic condition before using the theorem.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Parseval identity may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Functional Analysis I, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Parseval identity.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Parseval identity becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
