    "id": "ug-year-4-honours__differential-geometry-i__curves-and-surfaces__gaussian-curvature",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 4 / Honours",
    "chapter": "Differential Geometry I",
    "topic": "Curves and Surfaces",
    "subtopic": "Gaussian curvature",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 4 / Honours / Differential Geometry I / Curves and Surfaces</p><h2>Gaussian curvature</h2><p>Gaussian curvature is a specific idea in Curves and Surfaces.</p>\n<p>Gaussian curvature describes a random object, estimator, limit, or learning guarantee.</p>\n<p>The assumptions on data generation and dependence decide what can be concluded.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Gaussian curvature</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Gaussian curvature describes a random object, estimator, limit, or learning guarantee.</p>\n<p>The assumptions on data generation and dependence decide what can be concluded.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Gaussian curvature</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Gaussian curvature, use a small sample or two-state random process. Compute one probability, expectation, estimate, or bound and state the assumption used.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Gaussian curvature may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Differential Geometry I, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Gaussian curvature.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Gaussian curvature becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Gaussian curvature is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Gaussian curvature is controlled by convergence mode, dependence structure, model class, and loss or risk.</p>\n<p>These assumptions decide the valid theorem.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Gaussian curvature, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
