  {
    "sourceRow": 1399,
    "id": "ug-year-4-honours__number-theory__elementary-and-algebraic-number-theory__euler-s-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 4 / Honours",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory",
    "subtopic": "Euler's theorem",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 4 / Honours / Number Theory / Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory</p><h2>Euler&#x27;s theorem</h2><p>Euler&#x27;s theorem is a specific idea in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.</p>\n<p>Euler&#x27;s theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Euler&#x27;s theorem</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Euler&#x27;s theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Euler&#x27;s theorem</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For n=10, phi(10)=4 and gcd(3,10)=1. So 3^4=81 is congruent to 1 modulo 10.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Euler&#x27;s theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Euler&#x27;s theorem.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Euler&#x27;s theorem becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Euler&#x27;s theorem is about one main check: Euler&#x27;s theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.</li><li>Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This is Lagrange&#x27;s theorem in the unit group modulo n.</p>\n<p>The coprimality condition is essential.</p>\n<p>This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Euler&#x27;s theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909830
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1400,
    "id": "ug-year-4-honours__number-theory__elementary-and-algebraic-number-theory__fermat-s-little-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 4 / Honours",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory",
    "subtopic": "Fermat's Little Theorem",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">UG Year 4 / Honours / Number Theory / Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory</p><h2>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</h2><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem is a specific idea in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.</p>\n<p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For p=7 and a=3, 3^6=729, and 729 leaves remainder 1 modulo 7.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem is about one main check: Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.</li><li>Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The theorem is Lagrange&#x27;s theorem applied to the multiplicative group of nonzero residues modulo p.</p>\n<p>This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Fermat&#x27;s Little Theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786273909830
  },
