    "id": "pg-msc-core__functional-analysis__operator-theory__duality",
    "classLevel": "PG / MSc Core",
    "chapter": "Functional Analysis",
    "topic": "Operator Theory",
    "subtopic": "Duality",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">PG / MSc Core / Functional Analysis / Operator Theory</p><h2>Duality</h2><p>Duality is a specific idea in Operator Theory.</p>\n<p>Duality is a method or condition for choosing the best feasible option.</p>\n<p>Feasibility, objective function, and assumptions determine the correct result.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Duality</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Duality is a method or condition for choosing the best feasible option.</p>\n<p>Feasibility, objective function, and assumptions determine the correct result.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Duality</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Duality, minimize a simple function such as x^2 subject to one clear constraint. Check feasibility first, then apply the method or condition.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Duality may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Functional Analysis, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Duality.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Duality becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Duality is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Duality connects optimality conditions with geometry of feasible sets.</p>\n<p>Convexity, smoothness, or discreteness changes both algorithm and guarantee.</p></section></div>",
