    "topic": "Prime Distribution",
    "subtopic": "Dirichlet series",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">PG / MSc Electives / Analytic Number Theory / Prime Distribution</p><h2>Dirichlet series</h2><p>Dirichlet series is a specific idea in Prime Distribution.</p>\n<p>Dirichlet series studies integer or modular structure using divisibility, congruence, factorization, or arithmetic functions.</p>\n<p>Conditions like primality and coprimality matter.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Dirichlet series</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Dirichlet series studies integer or modular structure using divisibility, congruence, factorization, or arithmetic functions.</p>\n<p>Conditions like primality and coprimality matter.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Dirichlet series</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Dirichlet series, use small integers such as 5, 7, 10, or 12. Check divisibility or congruence conditions before applying the theorem.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Dirichlet series may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Analytic Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Dirichlet series.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Dirichlet series becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Dirichlet series is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Dirichlet series is shaped by arithmetic hypotheses such as primality, coprimality, local conditions, and factorization.</p>\n<p>Removing one can create counterexamples.</p></section></div>",
