  {
    "sourceRow": 1666,
    "id": "pg-msc-electives__cryptography__mathematical-cryptography__discrete-logarithm",
    "classLevel": "PG / MSc Electives",
    "chapter": "Cryptography",
    "topic": "Mathematical Cryptography",
    "subtopic": "Discrete logarithm",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">PG / MSc Electives / Cryptography / Mathematical Cryptography</p><h2>Discrete logarithm</h2><p>A discrete logarithm asks for the exponent that produces a target value in modular arithmetic.</p>\n<p>The idea is used in cryptography because exponentiation can be easy while reversing it can be hard.</p>\n<p>A solution exists only when the target lies in the subgroup generated by the base.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Discrete logarithm</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A discrete logarithm exists only inside the subgroup generated by the base, and exponents repeat modulo the base&#x27;s order.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Discrete logarithm</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Modulo 7, powers of 3 are 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1. Since 2 appears at exponent 2, x=2 is a solution.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If h is not in the subgroup generated by g, then no discrete logarithm to base g exists.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Cryptographic groups choose large orders so reversing g^x to find x is computationally hard.</li><li>Visual Model: Make a cycle table of powers of g and mark where the target h appears.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Discrete logarithm becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Think of powers of g as a repeating cycle.</li><li>A discrete logarithm asks where the target value appears in that cycle.</li><li>The cycle length is the order of g.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Group-theoretically, the problem is solved inside the cyclic subgroup generated by g.</p>\n<p>For base 3 modulo 7, the subgroup has order 6, so exponents are equivalent modulo 6.</p>\n<p>This order, not the modulus 7 itself, controls repeated exponents.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Modulo 7, list the powers of 3 until they repeat.</li><li>Which exponent solves 3^x congruent to 4 modulo 7, and what is the order of 3?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786274806491
  },
  {
