    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Post-quantum mathematics introduction, choose a tiny input set such as strings of length at most 3. State which strings are accepted, rejected, or hard to decide, then explain the rule.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Post-quantum mathematics introduction may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Cryptography, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Post-quantum mathematics introduction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Post-quantum mathematics introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
