    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">PG / MSc Electives / Financial Mathematics / Quantitative Finance</p><h2>Black-Scholes model</h2><p>Black-Scholes model is a specific idea in Quantitative Finance.</p>\n<p>Black-Scholes model models choices, prices, risk, or strategic behavior with explicit assumptions.</p>\n<p>The conclusion is valid only for the market, payoff, or information structure stated.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Black-Scholes model</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Black-Scholes model models choices, prices, risk, or strategic behavior with explicit assumptions.</p>\n<p>The conclusion is valid only for the market, payoff, or information structure stated.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Black-Scholes model</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Black-Scholes model, use two agents, two prices, or a one-period payoff. State the information and compute the resulting choice, value, or risk.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Black-Scholes model may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Financial Mathematics, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Black-Scholes model.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Black-Scholes model becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Black-Scholes model is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Black-Scholes model depends on equilibrium, information, admissible strategies, and risk assumptions.</p>\n<p>Changing these assumptions can change the theorem or price.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>For Black-Scholes model, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
