    "topic": "Advanced Analytic Number Theory",
    "subtopic": "Zero-free regions",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">PhD / Research / Pure Mathematics - Number Theory / Advanced Analytic Number Theory</p><h2>Zero-free regions</h2><p>Zero-free regions is a specific idea in Advanced Analytic Number Theory.</p>\n<p>Zero-free regions studies integer or modular structure using divisibility, congruence, factorization, or arithmetic functions.</p>\n<p>Conditions like primality and coprimality matter.</p>\n<p>It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Zero-free regions</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Zero-free regions studies integer or modular structure using divisibility, congruence, factorization, or arithmetic functions.</p>\n<p>Conditions like primality and coprimality matter.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Zero-free regions</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Zero-free regions, use small integers such as 5, 7, 10, or 12. Check divisibility or congruence conditions before applying the theorem.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Zero-free regions may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Pure Mathematics - Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Zero-free regions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Zero-free regions becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Zero-free regions is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Zero-free regions is shaped by arithmetic hypotheses such as primality, coprimality, local conditions, and factorization.</p>\n<p>Removing one can create counterexamples.</p></section></div>",
