    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Reaction-diffusion systems, define two variables such as population and time. Write how one step changes the variables, then interpret the result biologically.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Reaction-diffusion systems may not apply even when the notation looks similar.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: In Applied Mathematics - PDE and Scientific Computing, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.</li><li>Visual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Reaction-diffusion systems.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Reaction-diffusion systems becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Reaction-diffusion systems is easiest when you attach it to one small example.</li><li>Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Reaction-diffusion systems becomes deeper when parameters are identifiable, stable, and biologically interpretable.</p>\n<p>Model structure can matter more than numerical fitting.</p></section></div>",
