    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Propositional logic with modus ponens is a simple formal system.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Remove one required assumption and the conclusion may fail, even if the notation still looks familiar.\nExample 3 - Application: This concept helps researchers decide whether a proof, model, or algorithm is valid in Proof Theory.\nVisual Model: Use formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines to see the structure before writing formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Formal systems should be learned by asking three questions. What object is studied? What assumption is needed? What conclusion is allowed?",
    "advancedExplanation": "It makes proof checking mechanical in principle. At PhD level, this topic is valuable because its hypotheses, counterexamples, and canonical examples guide current research.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Formal systems, state the main definition or theorem, give one valid example, and explain one assumption that cannot be ignored.",
