    "topic": "Proof Theory",
    "subtopic": "Consistency",
    "introduction": "Consistency is a research-level idea in Proof Theory. It gives exact language for advanced mathematical objects or methods. Learning it helps students separate correct reasoning from similar-looking but false claims.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Consistency means a formal system does not prove a contradiction.\n\nHow It Works: First identify the objects involved. Next check the assumptions and the allowed operations. Then apply the definition only within that valid setting.\n\nWhy It Works: The assumptions control the conclusion. If a condition is removed, a counterexample may satisfy the words but fail the result.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Mathematical Logic and Foundations for proofs, models, algorithms, and research questions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the concept as a slogan or formula without checking its setting.\n\nCorrect idea: Write the object, assumption, conclusion, and one test example before using it.\n\nDefinition: Consistency means a formal system does not prove a contradiction.\n\nWorked Example: If a system proves both P and not P, it is inconsistent.\n\nVerification: Check the hypotheses, compare with the example, and test a simple boundary case.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines. Look for what remains invariant and what changes.\n\nMemory Trick: Use O-A-C: Object, Assumption, Conclusion.\n\n**Key Point:** Consistency means a formal system does not prove a contradiction.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a system proves both P and not P, it is inconsistent.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Remove one required assumption and the conclusion may fail, even if the notation still looks familiar.\nExample 3 - Application: This concept helps researchers decide whether a proof, model, or algorithm is valid in Proof Theory.\nVisual Model: Use formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines to see the structure before writing formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Consistency should be learned by asking three questions. What object is studied? What assumption is needed? What conclusion is allowed?",
    "advancedExplanation": "Consistency is central because inconsistent systems prove everything in classical logic. At PhD level, this topic is valuable because its hypotheses, counterexamples, and canonical examples guide current research.",
