    "introduction": "Rice theorem is a research-level idea in Computability. It gives exact language for advanced mathematical objects or methods. Learning it helps students separate correct reasoning from similar-looking but false claims.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Rice's theorem says every nontrivial semantic property of computable functions is undecidable.\n\nHow It Works: First identify the objects involved. Next check the assumptions and the allowed operations. Then apply the theorem only within that valid setting.\n\nWhy It Works: The assumptions control the conclusion. If a condition is removed, a counterexample may satisfy the words but fail the result.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Mathematical Logic and Foundations for proofs, models, algorithms, and research questions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the concept as a slogan or formula without checking its setting.\n\nCorrect idea: Write the object, assumption, conclusion, and one test example before using it.\n\nTheorem: Rice's theorem says every nontrivial semantic property of computable functions is undecidable.\n\nWorked Example: Whether a program computes the zero function is undecidable in general.\n\nVerification: Check the hypotheses, compare with the example, and test a simple boundary case.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines. Look for what remains invariant and what changes.\n\nMemory Trick: Use O-A-C: Object, Assumption, Conclusion.\n\n**Key Point:** Rice's theorem says every nontrivial semantic property of computable functions is undecidable.",
