    "subtopic": "NP-completeness",
    "introduction": "NP-completeness is an advanced idea in Theoretical Computer Science. It gives precise language for a research question, model, proof, or method. Learning it helps students judge claims carefully instead of trusting vague similarity.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: An NP-complete problem is in NP and every NP problem reduces to it in polynomial time.\n\nHow It Works: First state the objects. Next write the assumptions clearly. Then use the definition only when those assumptions are satisfied.\n\nWhy It Works: Research mathematics depends on exact conditions. If a condition is missing, the same method can give a false conclusion.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Discrete Mathematics and Theoretical CS for modelling, algorithms, proofs, communication, or research design.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a powerful idea without checking its definitions, limits, or data assumptions.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the setting, test a small example, and look for a counterexample before generalising.\n\nDefinition: An NP-complete problem is in NP and every NP problem reduces to it in polynomial time.\n\nWorked Example: SAT was the first NP-complete problem by Cook-Levin theorem.\n\nVerification: Compare with a known simple case, check assumptions, and confirm the conclusion follows.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture machines, languages, reductions, circuits, and resource tables. Notice which parts are inputs, rules, outputs, and limitations.\n\nMemory Trick: Use S-A-E: Setting, Assumption, Example.\n\n**Key Point:** An NP-complete problem is in NP and every NP problem reduces to it in polynomial time.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: SAT was the first NP-complete problem by Cook-Levin theorem.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Change one assumption and ask whether the conclusion still holds. If not, the assumption was essential.\nExample 3 - Application: This idea helps researchers make precise models, proofs, algorithms, or communication choices in Theoretical Computer Science.\nVisual Model: Use machines, languages, reductions, circuits, and resource tables to keep the concept concrete before using formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "NP-completeness is not just a name. Learn the setting, the needed assumption, and one example. That makes the research idea easier to trust and use.",
    "advancedExplanation": "If one NP-complete problem is in P, then P equals NP. At PhD level, the main skill is knowing the hypotheses, standard examples, limitations, and how the topic connects to nearby research areas.",
