    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Regularisation stabilizes ill-posed problems by adding constraints or penalties.\n\nHow It Works: First state the objects. Next write the assumptions clearly. Then use the definition only when those assumptions are satisfied.\n\nWhy It Works: Research mathematics depends on exact conditions. If a condition is missing, the same method can give a false conclusion.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Interdisciplinary Mathematics for modelling, algorithms, proofs, communication, or research design.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a powerful idea without checking its definitions, limits, or data assumptions.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the setting, test a small example, and look for a counterexample before generalising.\n\nDefinition: Regularisation stabilizes ill-posed problems by adding constraints or penalties.\n\nWorked Example: Tikhonov regularisation minimizes data error plus lambda times solution norm squared.\n\nVerification: Compare with a known simple case, check assumptions, and confirm the conclusion follows.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture operators, graphs, measurements, reconstructions, and uncertainty bands. Notice which parts are inputs, rules, outputs, and limitations.\n\nMemory Trick: Use S-A-E: Setting, Assumption, Example.\n\n**Key Point:** Regularisation stabilizes ill-posed problems by adding constraints or penalties.",
