    "introduction": "Computational conjectures is an advanced idea in Research Design and Communication. It gives precise language for a research question, model, proof, or method. Learning it helps students judge claims carefully instead of trusting vague similarity.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Computational conjectures arise from patterns found through exact or numerical computation.\n\nHow It Works: First state the objects. Next write the assumptions clearly. Then use the principle only when those assumptions are satisfied.\n\nWhy It Works: Research mathematics depends on exact conditions. If a condition is missing, the same method can give a false conclusion.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Research Practice for modelling, algorithms, proofs, communication, or research design.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a powerful idea without checking its definitions, limits, or data assumptions.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the setting, test a small example, and look for a counterexample before generalising.\n\nPrinciple: Computational conjectures arise from patterns found through exact or numerical computation.\n\nWorked Example: Checking many graph cases may suggest an extremal formula.\n\nVerification: Compare with a known simple case, check assumptions, and confirm the conclusion follows.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture papers, theorem statements, proof maps, examples, and reproducibility checklists. Notice which parts are inputs, rules, outputs, and limitations.\n\nMemory Trick: Use S-A-E: Setting, Assumption, Example.\n\n**Key Point:** Computational conjectures arise from patterns found through exact or numerical computation.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Checking many graph cases may suggest an extremal formula.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Change one assumption and ask whether the conclusion still holds. If not, the assumption was essential.\nExample 3 - Application: This idea helps researchers make precise models, proofs, algorithms, or communication choices in Research Design and Communication.\nVisual Model: Use papers, theorem statements, proof maps, examples, and reproducibility checklists to keep the concept concrete before using formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Computational conjectures is not just a name. Learn the setting, the needed assumption, and one example. That makes the research idea easier to trust and use.",
