    "introduction": "Numerical experimentation is an advanced idea in Research Design and Communication. It gives precise language for a research question, model, proof, or method. Learning it helps students judge claims carefully instead of trusting vague similarity.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Numerical experimentation studies mathematical questions using approximate computation.\n\nHow It Works: First state the objects. Next write the assumptions clearly. Then use the method only when those assumptions are satisfied.\n\nWhy It Works: Research mathematics depends on exact conditions. If a condition is missing, the same method can give a false conclusion.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Research Practice for modelling, algorithms, proofs, communication, or research design.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a powerful idea without checking its definitions, limits, or data assumptions.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the setting, test a small example, and look for a counterexample before generalising.\n\nMethod: Numerical experimentation studies mathematical questions using approximate computation.\n\nWorked Example: Simulating a PDE may reveal pattern formation before a theorem exists.\n\nVerification: Compare with a known simple case, check assumptions, and confirm the conclusion follows.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture papers, theorem statements, proof maps, examples, and reproducibility checklists. Notice which parts are inputs, rules, outputs, and limitations.\n\nMemory Trick: Use S-A-E: Setting, Assumption, Example.\n\n**Key Point:** Numerical experimentation studies mathematical questions using approximate computation.",
